{"id":210227773515836,"type":"genre","name":"El house de Chicago: WBMX y el Power Plant","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":15,"info":{"img":null},"own_scenes":[{"id":634,"name":"El house de Chicago: WBMX y el Power Plant","book":{"id":9,"slug":"TechnoRebels_Sicko","title":"Techno Rebels: The Renegades of Electronic Funk","author":"Dan Sicko","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/TechnoRebels/","families":{"raices":{"name":"Raíces: Kraftwerk, electro, funk, italo y new wave","color":"#a78bfa"},"fiestas":{"name":"Detroit antes del techno: clubes sociales y fiestas de instituto","color":"#d97706"},"radio":{"name":"Radio, clubes y DJs: Mojo, Ken Collier, The Wizard, Music Institute","color":"#facc15"},"pioneros":{"name":"Los pioneros: Atkins, May, Saunderson y la primera ola","color":"#ef4444"},"house":{"name":"House: Chicago, Nueva York y el garaje","color":"#ec4899"},"europa":{"name":"Europa: rave británico, Bélgica, Berlín, Países Bajos","color":"#38bdf8"},"underground":{"name":"Segunda ola de Detroit: UR, Plus 8, Carl Craig","color":"#16a34a"},"electro":{"name":"Electro de Detroit, Drexciya y ghettotech","color":"#2dd4bf"},"futuro":{"name":"Después: minimal, jazz, Ghostly, el festival de Detroit","color":"#a3e635"},"industria":{"name":"Industria, prensa y «electronica»","color":"#e5e7eb"}}},"family":"House: Chicago, Nueva York y el garaje","color":"#ec4899","start":1981,"end":1990,"place":"Chicago","desc":"En Chicago la emisora WBMX fue el gran semillero del house: Farley «Jackmaster» Funk fue el primero en tener allí un programa de mezclas, junto a Mario Diaz, Steve «Silk» Hurley, Mickey Oliver, Julian Perez y Ralphie Rosario, casi todos luego productores. Derrick May, que visitaba a su madre en Chicago, se sorprendió al oír que otros sentían esa música; en el Power Plant oyó a Frankie Knuckles y vio a la gente enamorarse bailando: «pinchar música con amor». De ahí sacó la profesionalidad y la dinámica que mezcló con el sonido electrónico de Atkins.","aka":["Chicago house","WBMX","Hot Mix 5","Power Plant","Trax Records","Frankie Knuckles"],"notes":[{"chapter":"Cap. 5: Off to Battle: Redefining the Detroit Underground, 1990–1995","text":"Li’l Louis (Louis Sims, hijo del batería de Chess Bobby Sims), tras el éxito de platino de «French Kiss» (1989), fue de los pocos del house que apostó por los discos largos en una multinacional (Epic): From the Mind of Li’l Louis (1989) y Journey with the Lonely (1992), un precedente pocas veces reconocido."}],"influences":[{"name":"Eurodisco e italo-disco: el «progressive» de Detroit","id":153482449187187,"type":"genre"},{"name":"Kraftwerk","id":156479957595892,"type":"artist"}],"refs":[{"ch":4,"q":"When I heard him play, and I saw the way people reacted, danced, and sang to the song—and fall in love with each other [to the music]—I knew this was something special. Not just being a DJ and playing music and being on a mission, but playing music with love.","para":"I also met some people and they took me to the Power Plant [where] I heard Frankie Knuckles play. Frankie was really a turning point in my life. . . . When I heard him play, and I saw the way people reacted, danced, and sang to the song—and fall in love with each other [to the music]—I knew this was something special. Not just being a DJ and playing music and being on a mission, but playing music with love. This vision of making a moment this euphoric . . . it changed me.","page":50,"pdf":49,"chapter":"Cap. 3: Time to Express: Techno’s First Artists Emerge, 1981–1989"}],"artists":[{"id":233744225406505,"type":"artist","name":"Frankie Knuckles","rating":null},{"id":35155532317504,"type":"artist","name":"Farley Jackmaster Funk","rating":null},{"id":73963058934413,"type":"artist","name":"Steve \"Silk\" Hurley","rating":null},{"id":195681381880865,"type":"artist","name":"Li’l Louis","rating":null},{"id":44019879296206,"type":"artist","name":"Ralphie Rosario","rating":null},{"id":3728473618078,"type":"artist","name":"Terry “Housemaster” Bal